1
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:04,540
You're here.

2
00:00:05,760 --> 00:00:07,380
Your organs are failing.

3
00:00:07,700 --> 00:00:09,300
And your death is drawing near.

4
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:13,440
But if you bribe the right politicians in the right order at the right time,

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:16,600
you can trade the nuclear warheads for medicine.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:20,080
And it's not even a crime.

7
00:01:40,364 --> 00:01:44,684
Unrepresentative Democracy Your government spends six hundred and four times

8
00:01:44,684 --> 00:01:47,764
more on weapons than on testing which medicines work.

9
00:01:48,524 --> 00:01:52,264
You could elect five hundred and thirty-five Gandhis and within two years

10
00:01:52,264 --> 00:01:56,924
they'd be funding nuclear weapons programs, because the system requires

11
00:01:56,924 --> 00:01:57,424
it.

12
00:01:58,084 --> 00:02:01,464
Princeton studied one thousand, seven hundred and seventy-nine of your policy

13
00:02:01,464 --> 00:02:05,464
decisions and found that public opinion has the same effect on legislation

14
00:02:05,464 --> 00:02:06,524
as a houseplant.

15
00:02:07,544 --> 00:02:11,964
This chapter explains why you can't vote your way out, and why that's actually

16
00:02:11,964 --> 00:02:15,364
fine, because the solution was never better politicians.

17
00:02:16,364 --> 00:02:20,384
Correlation between public opinion and policy outcomes:

18
00:02:20,624 --> 00:02:21,124
zero percent.

19
00:02:22,164 --> 00:02:25,744
On Wishonia, decisions are made by whoever has the best evidence.

20
00:02:26,444 --> 00:02:30,164
On Earth, decisions are made by whoever has the most papers to give the

21
00:02:30,264 --> 00:02:34,424
people who survive a popularity contest held every four years among people

22
00:02:34,424 --> 00:02:36,524
who were too busy to research the contestants.

23
00:02:37,404 --> 00:02:38,304
You call this "democracy,"

24
00:02:39,404 --> 00:02:43,744
which is Greek for "rule by the people," in the same way that "waterboarding"

25
00:02:43,764 --> 00:02:44,404
is about water.

26
00:02:45,484 --> 00:02:50,724
Your chance of deciding a presidential election is one in sixty million.

27
00:02:51,504 --> 00:02:53,864
You are more likely to die driving to the polls.

28
00:02:54,584 --> 00:02:56,264
The expected value of your vote:

29
00:02:56,964 --> 00:03:00,304
zero point zero zero zero three three eight dollars.

30
00:03:00,784 --> 00:03:05,084
The cost of casting it (time, transportation, parking, standing in line

31
00:03:05,084 --> 00:03:09,344
behind a man arguing about his I.D., a woman who brought her dog, and a

32
00:03:09,344 --> 00:03:13,984
voting machine manufactured by a company that donates to one of the candidates):

33
00:03:15,084 --> 00:03:17,124
about fifty dollars.

34
00:03:17,124 --> 00:03:19,964
You lose money voting.

35
00:03:19,964 --> 00:03:24,404
This is called "civic duty," which means "a financial loss you perform in

36
00:03:24,404 --> 00:03:25,164
formal clothing.".

37
00:03:26,364 --> 00:03:31,324
Corporations spend four point four billion dollars annually on lobbying.

38
00:03:31,804 --> 00:03:32,984
Their math works fine.

39
00:03:34,064 --> 00:03:36,484
What Democracy Looks Like From Space.

40
00:03:37,364 --> 00:03:41,224
Princeton University analyzed one thousand seven hundred and seventy-nine

41
00:03:41,224 --> 00:03:43,364
policy decisions over twenty years.

42
00:03:44,124 --> 00:03:48,123
The correlation between what ordinary voters want and what actually becomes

43
00:03:48,123 --> 00:03:48,623
law:

44
00:03:49,484 --> 00:03:50,004
zero percent.

45
00:03:50,724 --> 00:03:51,544
Not "low."

46
00:03:51,584 --> 00:03:52,544
Not "disappointing."

47
00:03:53,104 --> 00:03:53,604
Zero.

48
00:03:54,384 --> 00:03:58,464
Your democracy correlates with your preferences the way a coin flip correlates

49
00:03:58,484 --> 00:03:58,984
with your breakfast.

50
00:04:00,304 --> 00:04:04,204
When economic elites want something, it happens seventy-eight percent of

51
00:04:04,204 --> 00:04:04,704
the time.

52
00:04:04,964 --> 00:04:09,144
When you want something, it happens thirty percent of the time, regardless

53
00:04:09,144 --> 00:04:12,504
of whether zero percent or one hundred percent of you want it.

54
00:04:13,184 --> 00:04:14,184
The line is flat.

55
00:04:14,964 --> 00:04:18,144
A graph of your political influence looks like an E.K.G.

56
00:04:18,164 --> 00:04:19,144
of a dead person.

57
00:04:19,844 --> 00:04:23,584
You have the same political influence as your houseplant.

58
00:04:24,404 --> 00:04:25,224
Less, actually.

59
00:04:25,584 --> 00:04:29,564
Your houseplant produces oxygen, which at least one congressman needs.

60
00:04:30,604 --> 00:04:32,324
Your institutions already know this.

61
00:04:33,044 --> 00:04:36,164
You have a "Department of Defense" that mainly attacks people.

62
00:04:36,544 --> 00:04:40,444
A "Department of Health" that has not yet produced any observable health.

63
00:04:40,864 --> 00:04:42,784
And a "Department of Justice" that...

64
00:04:43,724 --> 00:04:44,484
well.

65
00:04:44,564 --> 00:04:45,704
You named your planet dirt.

66
00:04:46,084 --> 00:04:46,944
Naming is not your strength.

67
00:04:48,244 --> 00:04:52,304
The Mathematics of Why Nobody Fixes Anything.

68
00:04:52,304 --> 00:04:54,244
Concentrated Benefits vs.

69
00:04:54,244 --> 00:04:55,244
Diffuse Costs.

70
00:04:56,284 --> 00:05:01,084
Sugar subsidies cost Americans three point five billion dollars per year.

71
00:05:01,664 --> 00:05:03,044
That's ten dollars per person.

72
00:05:03,764 --> 00:05:07,644
Will you take a day off work, drive to your capitol, and stand in a hallway

73
00:05:07,644 --> 00:05:08,924
to save ten dollars?

74
00:05:09,404 --> 00:05:09,904
No.

75
00:05:10,244 --> 00:05:13,904
Will sugar companies spend millions to keep their three point five billion

76
00:05:13,904 --> 00:05:14,444
dollars?

77
00:05:14,944 --> 00:05:15,564
Obviously.

78
00:05:16,144 --> 00:05:16,664
That's it.

79
00:05:17,184 --> 00:05:21,224
That is the entire explanation for why your government does what it does.

80
00:05:21,864 --> 00:05:22,604
One paragraph.

81
00:05:23,444 --> 00:05:25,414
Everything else in this chapter is details.

82
00:05:25,824 --> 00:05:27,524
The details are where the crime hides.

83
00:05:28,624 --> 00:05:31,344
On Wishonia, we call this "beige crime":

84
00:05:31,924 --> 00:05:35,184
theft so boring that nobody investigates it.

85
00:05:35,224 --> 00:05:38,984
Your best criminals discovered long ago that the boredom threshold of the

86
00:05:38,984 --> 00:05:42,224
average citizen is approximately ten dollars.

87
00:05:43,024 --> 00:05:45,704
Steal more than that from one person and they'll complain.

88
00:05:46,244 --> 00:05:50,544
Steal ten dollars from three hundred and thirty million people and you'll

89
00:05:50,544 --> 00:05:52,084
receive an industry award.

90
00:05:52,744 --> 00:05:56,664
The pattern repeats everywhere, like wallpaper in a prison:

91
00:05:56,664 --> 00:06:00,263
Pharma receives five hundred billion dollars in profits (concentrated),

92
00:06:00,924 --> 00:06:05,644
patients pay one thousand five hundred dollars each (diffuse), and the winner

93
00:06:05,644 --> 00:06:07,774
is pharma (obviously).

94
00:06:08,924 --> 00:06:12,884
Banks in two thousand and eight received a seven hundred billion dollar

95
00:06:12,884 --> 00:06:14,484
bailout (concentrated),

96
00:06:15,184 --> 00:06:19,184
taxpayers paid two thousand one hundred dollars each (diffuse),

97
00:06:19,844 --> 00:06:22,424
and the winner is banks (obviously).

98
00:06:23,624 --> 00:06:28,484
Sugar receives three point five billion dollars in subsidies (concentrated),

99
00:06:29,324 --> 00:06:32,484
you pay ten dollars (not worth a phone call),

100
00:06:33,144 --> 00:06:35,934
and the winner is sugar (obviously).

101
00:06:37,464 --> 00:06:39,644
The small group hires lobbyists.

102
00:06:40,424 --> 00:06:41,804
You hire nobody.

103
00:06:42,424 --> 00:06:46,924
This is called "representative democracy," which represents you in the same

104
00:06:46,924 --> 00:06:49,564
way a casino represents financial planning.

105
00:06:51,124 --> 00:06:52,984
The Death Spiral:

106
00:06:53,024 --> 00:06:56,884
How Two Choices Kill three hundred and thirty million Options.

107
00:06:58,344 --> 00:07:04,104
A human named James Surowiecki proved that crowds outperform experts under

108
00:07:04,104 --> 00:07:05,984
four conditions:

109
00:07:05,984 --> 00:07:06,824
diversity of opinion,

110
00:07:07,744 --> 00:07:10,244
independence of judgment, decentralization,

111
00:07:10,944 --> 00:07:13,584
and a good aggregation mechanism.

112
00:07:14,584 --> 00:07:17,364
On Wishonia, we learned this by watching fish.

113
00:07:18,204 --> 00:07:22,764
Your political scientists spent two hundred years and several revolutions

114
00:07:22,764 --> 00:07:24,724
to reach the same conclusion.

115
00:07:25,264 --> 00:07:26,584
The fish were faster.

116
00:07:27,784 --> 00:07:30,604
Your democracy has three of these conditions.

117
00:07:31,024 --> 00:07:34,384
It systematically destroys the most important one.

118
00:07:34,824 --> 00:07:39,364
A human named Charles Mackay described the result in eighteen forty-one:

119
00:07:40,024 --> 00:07:43,204
the wisdom of crowds becomes the madness of crowds.

120
00:07:43,964 --> 00:07:44,984
He was ignored,

121
00:07:45,884 --> 00:07:46,384
obviously.

122
00:07:47,204 --> 00:07:51,424
Independence means each person decides based on their own knowledge, not

123
00:07:51,424 --> 00:07:52,864
by watching everyone else.

124
00:07:53,344 --> 00:07:55,904
Your voting system annihilates this.

125
00:07:56,024 --> 00:07:58,824
You have three hundred and thirty million people.

126
00:07:59,564 --> 00:08:00,924
You choose between two of them,

127
00:08:01,704 --> 00:08:06,724
pre-selected by a process optimized for fundraising, name recognition, and

128
00:08:06,724 --> 00:08:10,884
not offending people who vote in primaries (which is approximately nine

129
00:08:10,884 --> 00:08:12,064
people per state,

130
00:08:12,704 --> 00:08:13,724
seven of whom are angry).

131
00:08:14,464 --> 00:08:16,724
Then you don't even vote for the one you like.

132
00:08:17,124 --> 00:08:19,484
You vote against the one you fear.

133
00:08:19,824 --> 00:08:21,564
This is called "strategic voting,"

134
00:08:22,204 --> 00:08:25,144
which means your ballot doesn't measure what you want.

135
00:08:25,784 --> 00:08:29,244
It measures what you think other people will do.

136
00:08:29,424 --> 00:08:31,084
That is not independence.

137
00:08:31,804 --> 00:08:34,204
That is a herd.

138
00:08:34,284 --> 00:08:37,884
On Wishonia, we observed this same phenomenon in army ants.

139
00:08:39,324 --> 00:08:41,224
Army ants are nearly blind.

140
00:08:41,484 --> 00:08:45,984
They navigate by following pheromone trails laid by the ants ahead of them.

141
00:08:46,084 --> 00:08:50,064
When a foraging column gets separated from the main trail, the leading ants

142
00:08:50,064 --> 00:08:52,664
have nothing to follow, so they wander.

143
00:08:53,144 --> 00:08:55,584
But they're still laying pheromone as they walk.

144
00:08:56,364 --> 00:09:00,764
If the front of the column curves enough to cross its own trail, the leaders

145
00:09:00,764 --> 00:09:02,764
pick up that signal and follow it.

146
00:09:03,404 --> 00:09:04,444
The circle closes.

147
00:09:05,064 --> 00:09:10,564
Every ant is now both following and reinforcing the path that traps it.

148
00:09:11,324 --> 00:09:16,684
They march in a loop, sometimes hundreds of thousands strong, until they

149
00:09:16,684 --> 00:09:17,664
die of exhaustion.

150
00:09:18,684 --> 00:09:21,344
No individual ant is stupid.

151
00:09:21,444 --> 00:09:23,744
Each is following a perfectly rational rule:

152
00:09:24,204 --> 00:09:25,424
follow the strongest signal.

153
00:09:26,124 --> 00:09:27,664
The system is stupid.

154
00:09:28,404 --> 00:09:33,944
The circle emerges not from bad ants but from a structure where no ant can

155
00:09:33,944 --> 00:09:35,424
navigate independently.

156
00:09:35,684 --> 00:09:39,544
Your two-party system is a death spiral with bumper stickers.

157
00:09:40,244 --> 00:09:42,284
Pollsters tell you who's "electable."

158
00:09:42,824 --> 00:09:45,444
Media covers the two "real" candidates.

159
00:09:46,004 --> 00:09:48,604
Donors fund the two "viable" options.

160
00:09:49,184 --> 00:09:52,804
You vote for one of them because everyone else is voting for one of them

161
00:09:52,804 --> 00:09:55,544
because everyone else is voting for one of them.

162
00:09:56,164 --> 00:10:00,164
The best candidate out of three hundred and thirty million people could

163
00:10:00,164 --> 00:10:04,074
be standing right there, but if they're not wearing the right color tie,

164
00:10:04,624 --> 00:10:08,704
they're "throwing their vote away" (a phrase that only makes sense in a

165
00:10:08,704 --> 00:10:10,764
system designed to waste votes).

166
00:10:11,644 --> 00:10:12,184
The result:

167
00:10:12,684 --> 00:10:16,783
a nation of three hundred and thirty million, selecting from a talent pool

168
00:10:16,783 --> 00:10:18,783
of three hundred and thirty million,

169
00:10:19,564 --> 00:10:24,184
reliably produces a choice between two people that approximately nobody

170
00:10:24,184 --> 00:10:25,824
is enthusiastic about.

171
00:10:26,684 --> 00:10:29,804
On Wishonia, this would be classified as a math error.

172
00:10:30,384 --> 00:10:32,284
On Earth, it is classified as "freedom."

173
00:10:33,924 --> 00:10:35,824
Each ant follows the one ahead.

174
00:10:36,304 --> 00:10:37,604
The leader follows the last.

175
00:10:38,184 --> 00:10:40,144
They march in a circle until they die.

176
00:10:40,764 --> 00:10:45,444
Your two-party system works the same way, but the circle takes four years.

177
00:10:46,204 --> 00:10:49,144
(Clemzouzou sixty-nine, C.C. B.Y.

178
00:10:49,144 --> 00:10:49,644
S.A.

179
00:10:49,784 --> 00:10:50,284
four point zero).

180
00:10:51,244 --> 00:10:53,064
How Money Buys Your Government

181
00:10:53,984 --> 00:10:58,584
On most planets, giving someone papers so they'll do what you want is called

182
00:10:58,584 --> 00:10:59,344
"bribery."

183
00:10:59,724 --> 00:11:02,064
On Earth, it's called "campaign finance."

184
00:11:02,844 --> 00:11:05,364
The distinction matters, but not in the way you think.

185
00:11:06,164 --> 00:11:09,184
Campaign contributions don't directly buy how politicians vote.

186
00:11:09,544 --> 00:11:11,684
Only one in four studies even support that.

187
00:11:12,644 --> 00:11:16,004
The system is much more elegant than direct bribery.

188
00:11:16,604 --> 00:11:20,944
What money actually buys is which politicians exist in the first place:

189
00:11:21,564 --> 00:11:25,344
Ninety-five percent of House races since two thousand and four were won

190
00:11:25,344 --> 00:11:27,224
by whoever had more papers.

191
00:11:27,964 --> 00:11:32,364
The average winning House campaign costs two point seven nine million dollars

192
00:11:32,364 --> 00:11:36,864
(less than a nice apartment in Manhattan, and arguably less useful).

193
00:11:37,784 --> 00:11:42,604
The average winning Senate campaign costs twenty-six point five three million

194
00:11:42,604 --> 00:11:46,764
dollars (a senator costs more, presumably because there are fewer of them,

195
00:11:47,084 --> 00:11:47,724
like truffles).

196
00:11:48,504 --> 00:11:52,664
Why bribe a politician when you can manufacture one who already agrees with

197
00:11:52,664 --> 00:11:53,164
you?

198
00:11:53,624 --> 00:11:55,344
On Wishonia, we had a saying:

199
00:11:55,984 --> 00:11:59,254
"why train the dog when you can buy a dog that already sits?".

200
00:11:59,984 --> 00:12:01,224
For the ones already sitting,

201
00:12:01,984 --> 00:12:05,224
papers buy something more useful than votes:

202
00:12:05,224 --> 00:12:06,344
Access:

203
00:12:06,384 --> 00:12:07,944
Your call gets returned.

204
00:12:07,984 --> 00:12:09,654
Everyone else gets voicemail.

205
00:12:09,984 --> 00:12:12,264
(Voicemail is where democracy goes to die.).

206
00:12:13,064 --> 00:12:14,544
Agenda control:

207
00:12:14,644 --> 00:12:15,944
Your issue gets a hearing.

208
00:12:16,164 --> 00:12:17,724
Everyone else's gets a filing cabinet.

209
00:12:18,884 --> 00:12:20,124
Legislative language:

210
00:12:20,484 --> 00:12:24,364
The exact wording that creates the exact loopholes you need.

211
00:12:25,144 --> 00:12:25,944
Committee positions:

212
00:12:26,744 --> 00:12:30,764
Four hundred and fifty thousand dollars to party committees buys a seat

213
00:12:30,764 --> 00:12:32,584
where bills live or die.

214
00:12:33,344 --> 00:12:34,324
You don't buy the vote.

215
00:12:34,764 --> 00:12:38,594
You buy the voter, the agenda, and the room where the vote happens.

216
00:12:39,184 --> 00:12:41,064
Then the vote takes care of itself.

217
00:12:41,724 --> 00:12:45,764
This is like rigging a horse race by buying the horse, the jockey, the track,

218
00:12:46,064 --> 00:12:47,684
and the concept of racing.

219
00:12:48,624 --> 00:12:51,744
The Lobbying Return on Investment, or R.O.I.:

220
00:12:52,224 --> 00:12:54,024
Better Than Every Other Investment

221
00:12:54,904 --> 00:12:59,324
The most profitable investment on your planet is not stocks, real estate,

222
00:12:59,404 --> 00:13:00,744
or cryptocurrency.

223
00:13:00,804 --> 00:13:02,444
It's congressmen:

224
00:13:02,504 --> 00:13:06,864
Disease advocacy groups give one hundred million dollars in papers to lobbyists.

225
00:13:07,524 --> 00:13:11,284
They receive one point eight billion dollars in papers from the government.

226
00:13:11,944 --> 00:13:16,924
This represents a return of eighteen to one (one thousand seven hundred

227
00:13:16,924 --> 00:13:18,204
percent net).

228
00:13:18,224 --> 00:13:21,164
Your stock market returns about ten percent per year.

229
00:13:21,724 --> 00:13:24,764
Your congressmen return seventeen hundred percent.

230
00:13:25,404 --> 00:13:26,374
This is not a metaphor.

231
00:13:27,184 --> 00:13:28,514
These are the actual numbers.

232
00:13:29,084 --> 00:13:32,164
If your stockbroker offered you seventeen hundred percent, you'd call the

233
00:13:32,164 --> 00:13:32,664
police.

234
00:13:33,064 --> 00:13:36,044
When your congressman offers it, you call it "advocacy."

235
00:13:36,544 --> 00:13:40,364
The only reason more people don't invest in congressmen is that the minimum

236
00:13:40,364 --> 00:13:44,824
buy-in requires a trade association, which requires being rich (which is

237
00:13:44,824 --> 00:13:45,664
the entire point).

238
00:13:46,404 --> 00:13:50,864
The eighteen-to-one return means your disease funding is allocated by lobbying

239
00:13:50,864 --> 00:13:53,174
power, not by who's dying.

240
00:13:53,604 --> 00:13:57,364
Diseases with celebrity spokespeople and well-funded advocacy groups get

241
00:13:57,364 --> 00:13:58,704
research budgets.

242
00:13:58,704 --> 00:14:03,534
Diseases that kill quietly, in populations too poor or too young to organize,

243
00:14:04,084 --> 00:14:04,584
get nothing.

244
00:14:05,284 --> 00:14:09,284
If your disease can't hire a lobbying firm, it isn't a real disease.

245
00:14:09,724 --> 00:14:12,064
It's just dying, which doesn't have a PAC.

246
00:14:13,024 --> 00:14:13,784
Your Congressman:

247
00:14:14,264 --> 00:14:17,744
A Professional Fundraiser Who Occasionally Dabbles in Legislation.

248
00:14:18,644 --> 00:14:21,584
I studied how your elected representatives spend their time.

249
00:14:22,004 --> 00:14:24,144
I assumed they spent it representing.

250
00:14:24,944 --> 00:14:25,744
I was incorrect.

251
00:14:26,544 --> 00:14:29,904
How congressmen spend a ten-hour workday:

252
00:14:29,904 --> 00:14:33,164
They spend four to five hours calling rich people for papers.

253
00:14:34,004 --> 00:14:37,144
Committee hearings, floor votes, and meetings take four hours.

254
00:14:37,704 --> 00:14:41,364
Reading the laws they're about to vote on gets whatever's left (roughly

255
00:14:41,364 --> 00:14:41,904
one hour).

256
00:14:42,644 --> 00:14:45,944
The laws that need reading require six hours' worth of time.

257
